# Surface: nushell — the daily driver.
# Schema, verify kinds and writing rules: see README.md in this directory.
# Entries are grouped by topic in the order topics.nuon declares.
#
# These entries describe the configuration being BUILT, and the keybinding
# names in `verify` were read off the live nushell config so they are real
# names rather than invented ones. Four behaviours are documented as they are
# specified rather than as the live implementation currently does them, all
# four recorded on the delivery corrections backlog: `ls -D` (the live version
# uses a `du` flag macOS does not have), the git-log commit decode (dead in
# the live finder), the recent-dirs channel name, and whether finder picks
# reach the recents log (they do not yet). If one of them is still wrong when
# the shell lands, the entry is wrong too — fix both in the same change.
[
    # ---------------------------------------------------------------- navigate
    {
        cmd: "z <query>"
        title: "Jump to a directory by a fragment of its path"
        use: "Type `z` and any part of the path — `z dot` reaches ~/dev/dotfiles. Zoxide picks the best frecency match and you land there; the listing prints itself. A single argument that resolves to an existing *file* opens it in nvim instead of jumping, so `z notes.md` edits."
        topic: "navigate"
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["zi", "zz", "<word>"]
        why: "Every jump goes through the `cd` funnel, so the start dir, the dirstack and the recents log update no matter which route you took. A jump that does not move PWD logs nothing — a failed `z` leaves no trace."
        verify: [{kind: "command", name: "z"}]
        source: ".mi/prd/04-shell/03-zoxide/prd.md"
    }
    {
        cmd: "zi"
        title: "Pick a directory from zoxide interactively"
        use: "Run `zi` with no argument: the zoxide database opens as a picker, ordered by frecency. Choose a row and you land there."
        topic: "navigate"
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["cdi", "z <query>"]
        verify: [{kind: "command", name: "zi"}]
        source: ".mi/prd/04-shell/03-zoxide/prd.md"
    }
    {
        cmd: "cdi"
        title: "Pick a directory from zoxide interactively"
        use: "Alias for `zi` — the same picker under the name muscle memory reaches for."
        topic: "navigate"
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["zi"]
        verify: [{kind: "alias", name: "cdi"}]
        source: ".mi/prd/04-shell/02-aliases-utilities/prd.md"
    }
    {
        cmd: "zz"
        title: "Toggle back to the directory you came from"
        use: "Run `zz` — it is `cd -`. Run it again to come back. It pairs with the bare-word jump: word, look, `zz`."
        topic: "navigate"
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["<word>"]
        verify: [{kind: "command", name: "zz"}]
        source: ".mi/prd/04-shell/03-zoxide/prd.md"
    }
    {
        cmd: "zl <query>"
        title: "Jump to a directory and list it"
        use: "Same query as `z`, then `la` on arrival — for when you want the hidden files too."
        topic: "navigate"
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["z <query>", "l / ll / la"]
        verify: [{kind: "command", name: "zl"}]
        source: ".mi/prd/04-shell/03-zoxide/prd.md"
    }
    {
        cmd: "zc <query>"
        title: "Jump to a directory and start Claude there"
        use: "Same query as `z`, then `cc` in the directory you land in."
        topic: "navigate"
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["z <query>", "cc [...args]"]
        verify: [{kind: "command", name: "zc"}]
        source: ".mi/prd/04-shell/03-zoxide/prd.md"
    }
    {
        cmd: "<word>"
        title: "Jump by typing a directory name on its own"
        use: "Type a bare word that is not a command — `dotfiles` — and press enter. If zoxide knows a directory matching it you land there with a cleared screen; the fresh prompt in the new directory is the confirmation. If it knows nothing, you get the ordinary command-not-found."
        topic: "navigate"
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["z <query>", "zz"]
        why: "The jump happens in `pre_execution` because that is the only hook where a cd persists, and the screen is cleared in `pre_prompt` to bury the command-not-found error that is already on its way. The trigger is deliberately narrow — first token unknown, no shell metacharacters, not path-shaped — so `./x` and `ls | nope` never jump. It queries `zoxide query --exclude $PWD` directly and only jumps on a real hit, because the funnel reads an empty argument as no argument at all and would otherwise take you HOME on every miss."
        verify: [{kind: "prose"}]
        source: ".mi/prd/04-shell/03-zoxide/prd.md"
    }
    {
        cmd: "cd <path>"
        title: "Enter a directory, creating it if it does not exist"
        use: "`cd` on a path that is not there offers to make it: one key to confirm, then you are in it. `cd` alone goes home, `cd -` toggles back. Everything else about it is the builtin."
        topic: "navigate"
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["mkcd", "z <query>"]
        why: "`cd` is aliased to the `mkcd` wrapper, which is the single funnel every kind of navigation reaches the shell through."
        verify: [{kind: "alias", name: "cd"}]
        source: ".mi/prd/04-shell/01-core-config/prd.md"
    }
    {
        cmd: "mkcd"
        title: "Understand the one funnel every move goes through"
        use: "Nothing to run — this is the shape of the shell. Real `cd`, zoxide jumps, picker jumps and the bare-word fallback all arrive at `mkcd`, and the PWD hook is the single place that reacts to a move."
        topic: "navigate"
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["cd <path>", "<word>", "Ctrl-Q"]
        why: "Because there is exactly one funnel and exactly one reaction point, four things stay true however you moved: the directory is recorded to `startdir.txt` so a new pane opens where you left off, it is pushed onto the dirstack that feeds the recent-dirs picker, the pick is logged to the recents list, and the listing prints. Scatter that per navigation command and one route always ends up forgetting one of the four."
        verify: [{kind: "prose"}]
        source: ".mi/prd/04-shell/prd.md"
    }
    {
        cmd: "ls"
        title: "List a directory as structured, decorated data"
        use: "Plain `ls`. Rows are sorted by type then modified time, so directories group together and the freshest thing sits nearest the prompt, and each row carries an icon. It is still a nushell table: `ls | where size > 1mb | sort-by modified` works."
        topic: "navigate"
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["l / ll / la", "ls -D"]
        why: "The builtin is captured as `core-ls` before being shadowed, because an alias target binds at parse time — without the capture the wrapper would call itself."
        verify: [{kind: "command", name: "ls"}]
        source: ".mi/prd/04-shell/06-listing/prd.md"
    }
    {
        cmd: "ls -D"
        title: "List with real recursive directory sizes"
        use: "Add `-D` to swap each directory's inode size for what it actually occupies on disk. One `du` spawn does the whole listing."
        topic: "navigate"
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["ls"]
        why: "Opt-in only, and it stays that way: a single `node_modules` would stall every listing in the tree if this were the default. It also cannot be spelled `du -sb` — macOS `du` has no `-b`, and the old implementation discarded the error, so every directory silently kept its inode size."
        verify: [{kind: "command", name: "ls"}]
        source: ".mi/prd/04-shell/06-listing/prd.md"
    }
    {
        cmd: "l / ll / la"
        title: "List short, long, or including dotfiles"
        use: "`l` is the quick one, `ll` the long form, `la` includes hidden files. `la` is also what runs by itself after every directory change."
        topic: "navigate"
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["ls"]
        verify: [{kind: "command", name: "l"}, {kind: "command", name: "ll"}, {kind: "command", name: "la"}]
        source: ".mi/prd/04-shell/06-listing/prd.md"
    }

    # -------------------------------------------------------------------- find
    {
        key: "Ctrl-Space / F1"
        title: "Open the finder and act on what you pick"
        use: "Press `Ctrl-Space` (or `F1`). The channel list opens first — type a few letters of the channel you want (`fil`, `dir`, `text`, `git-log`), enter, then pick a row. What happens next follows the type: a file opens in nvim, a directory cds the shell and lists it, a grep hit opens the editor on that line, a commit opens in `git show`. `Tab` multi-selects."
        topic: "find"
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["Ctrl-T", "Ctrl-Q", "finder"]
        why: "Enter has to be un-hijacked per run (`--keybindings 'enter=\"confirm_selection\"'`) because three channels bind it to an action of their own instead of confirming: `text` edits in place, `zoxide` cds in a nested shell, `recent-files` edits. Note that the `text` override is local to this config, not stock tv. tv panics outright without a TTY, so this and every other picker entry point is interactive-only by construction."
        verify: [{kind: "keybinding", name: "tv_remote"}, {kind: "keybinding", name: "tv_remote_f1"}]
        source: ".mi/prd/04-shell/04-television/prd.md"
    }
    {
        key: "Ctrl-T"
        title: "Insert a path into the line you are typing"
        use: "Mid-command, press `Ctrl-T`, pick a file or directory: it is inserted at the cursor, shell-quoted, and you carry on typing. Esc leaves the line exactly as it was. This is the fzf reflex, on television."
        topic: "find"
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["Ctrl-Space / F1"]
        why: "It reaches the channel remote by the same route `Ctrl-Space` does — both end up in `_finder_pick_channel` over `tv list-channels` — so a cable file you add for one key is on the other key too, with nothing to register twice."
        verify: [{kind: "keybinding", name: "finder_pick"}]
        source: ".mi/prd/04-shell/04-television/prd.md"
    }
    {
        key: "Ctrl-Q"
        title: "Reopen something you picked or jumped to recently"
        use: "Press `Ctrl-Q` for one list of everything recent across every channel — jumps, files opened via `z`, finder picks — newest first. `enter` opens it by type. `ctrl-r` replays instead: it cds to the directory the pick was made in and re-runs the channel it came from, so a grep result reopens the same search where you ran it. An empty log prints a hint rather than an empty picker."
        topic: "find"
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["Ctrl-Space / F1", "mkcd"]
        verify: [{kind: "keybinding", name: "quicklist"}]
        source: ".mi/prd/04-shell/07-quicklist/prd.md"
    }
    {
        cmd: "finder"
        title: "Run a picker and get typed nushell data back"
        use: "`finder` opens the channel remote; `finder --start files` opens one channel directly. The return value is data, not a string: a list of paths, `{file, line, text}` rows from a grep, `{hash, subject}` records from `git-log`. Pipe it like anything else."
        topic: "find"
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["Ctrl-Space / F1", "tv channel"]
        why: "Typed decode per channel is what lets one opener do the right thing for a file, a directory, a grep hit and a commit — and it is why new pickers are new channels plus a decode, never a new hand-rolled TUI."
        verify: [{kind: "command", name: "finder"}]
        source: ".mi/prd/04-shell/04-television/prd.md"
    }
    {
        cmd: "tv channel"
        title: "Understand what a channel is, and add one"
        use: "Nothing to run. A channel is a cable file under `~/.config/television` naming a source command that emits rows, plus how to preview one. Add a file, and it appears in the `Ctrl-Space` remote alongside the built-ins; teach `finder` how to decode its rows and every opener works on it too."
        topic: "find"
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["finder", "Ctrl-Space / F1"]
        why: "One rule keeps the picker surface from sprawling: television owns every picker screen. The curated set is files, dirs, text, zoxide, recent dirs (`recent-dirs`, fed by the dirstack), git-log, git-files, git-branch, env and quicklist — anything else migrates on demand. tv runs on the `default` ANSI theme so it inherits the terminal's palette instead of baking hex values."
        verify: [{kind: "prose"}]
        source: ".mi/prd/04-shell/04-television/prd.md"
    }
    {
        cmd: "grep"
        title: "Search file contents with ripgrep"
        use: "`grep <pattern>` is `rg <pattern>` — recursive by default, respecting ignore files. Reach for `rg` and `fd`, not `grep` and `find`."
        topic: "find"
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["Ctrl-Space / F1", "idioms"]
        why: "`rg` and `fd` share one ignore ruleset through `RIPGREP_CONFIG_PATH`, so a path hidden from one is hidden from both."
        verify: [{kind: "alias", name: "grep"}]
        source: ".mi/prd/04-shell/02-aliases-utilities/prd.md"
    }
    {
        cmd: "help --fuzzy"
        title: "Fuzzy-search the manual itself"
        use: "`help --fuzzy` opens every entry in this manual as a picker; type what you half-remember (`select`, `jump`, `capsule`) and the preview shows the full entry — title, use, why, related. `enter` prints that entry into the scrollback; `ctrl-o` opens the PRD it came from in `$EDITOR`. Without a TTY it degrades to `help <query>`."
        topic: "find"
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["help", "Ctrl-Space / F1"]
        verify: [{kind: "command", name: "help"}]
        source: ".mi/prd/06-help/03-browser/prd.md"
    }

    # ----------------------------------------------------------------- history
    {
        key: "Ctrl-R"
        title: "Search this directory's history"
        use: "Press `Ctrl-R`: a picker opens over the commands you have run *in this directory*, newest first and deduplicated, prefilled with whatever you had typed up to the cursor. Enter replaces the command line with the pick; Esc leaves the line alone."
        topic: "history"
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["Alt-R", "Up / Down"]
        why: "Directory-scoped is the default because a command worth repeating is nearly always one you ran here; the global list is one modifier away. The sqlite store runs with `isolation: false` so every pane contributes to one merged history."
        verify: [{kind: "keybinding", name: "hist_picker_local"}]
        source: ".mi/prd/04-shell/05-history/prd.md"
    }
    {
        key: "Alt-R"
        title: "Search the whole history, from anywhere"
        use: "Press `Alt-R` for television's own shell-history picker over every command from every directory. Enter puts the pick on the command line."
        topic: "history"
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["Ctrl-R", "Shift+Up / Shift+Down"]
        why: "`Alt`, not `Ctrl-Shift`. The kitty keyboard protocol stays off — it leaks `^[[?0u` through the WezTerm pty — and without it shift is indistinguishable on a control+letter chord, so `Ctrl-Shift-R` would arrive as plain `Ctrl-R` and shadow the local picker."
        verify: [{kind: "keybinding", name: "hist_picker_global"}]
        source: ".mi/prd/04-shell/05-history/prd.md"
    }
    {
        key: "Up / Down"
        title: "Step through this directory's history on the line"
        use: "With the cursor on the command line, `Up` walks back through this directory's commands and `Down` forward; typing anything resets you to the newest. No picker, no screen change."
        topic: "history"
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["Shift+Up / Shift+Down", "Ctrl-R"]
        why: "Both keys try `menuup`/`menudown` first in an `until` chain, so with a completion menu open the arrows still navigate the menu. And these bindings are appended *after* television's generated init, because reedline resolves last-entry-wins and tv's own Ctrl-R would otherwise take the key."
        verify: [{kind: "keybinding", name: "hist_up_local"}, {kind: "keybinding", name: "hist_down_local"}]
        source: ".mi/prd/04-shell/05-history/prd.md"
    }
    {
        key: "Shift+Up / Shift+Down"
        title: "Step through the whole history on the line"
        use: "Hold shift with the arrows for reedline's own global traversal — the same inline stepping, ignoring which directory you are in. Use it when the command you want was run somewhere else."
        topic: "history"
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["Up / Down", "Alt-R"]
        verify: [{kind: "keybinding", name: "hist_up_global"}, {kind: "keybinding", name: "hist_down_global"}]
        source: ".mi/prd/04-shell/05-history/prd.md"
    }

    # -------------------------------------------------------------------- edit
    {
        key: "Esc"
        title: "Close the menu, or clear the line"
        use: "Press `Esc` once: if a completion or history menu is open it closes and the line survives. With no menu open it clears the line, the way `Ctrl-C` would."
        topic: "edit"
        mode: "shell"
        verify: [{kind: "keybinding", name: "esc_clear"}]
        source: ".mi/prd/04-shell/01-core-config/prd.md"
    }
    {
        cmd: "cat <file>"
        title: "Dump a file with syntax highlighting and no pager"
        use: "`cat <file>` is `bat --paging=never`: highlighted, line-numbered, and it still pipes like `cat` because nothing waits for a key."
        topic: "edit"
        mode: "shell"
        verify: [{kind: "alias", name: "cat"}]
        source: ".mi/prd/04-shell/02-aliases-utilities/prd.md"
    }
    {
        cmd: "nv / vi"
        title: "Open Neovim"
        use: "Either name starts nvim. `$EDITOR` and `$VISUAL` are nvim too, so anything that opens an editor for you — the finder, `z` on a file, a commit message — lands in the same place."
        topic: "edit"
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["nn"]
        verify: [{kind: "alias", name: "nv"}, {kind: "alias", name: "vi"}]
        source: ".mi/prd/04-shell/02-aliases-utilities/prd.md"
    }
    {
        cmd: "nn"
        title: "Open the notes file"
        use: "`nn` opens `~/notes.md` in nvim, from wherever you are."
        topic: "edit"
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["nv / vi"]
        verify: [{kind: "alias", name: "nn"}]
        source: ".mi/prd/04-shell/02-aliases-utilities/prd.md"
    }
    {
        cmd: "cf <file>"
        title: "Copy a file's contents to the system clipboard"
        use: "`cf <file>` puts the whole file on the clipboard. The argument is `path`-typed, so Tab completes it. A missing file errors instead of copying nothing."
        topic: "edit"
        mode: "shell"
        why: "It picks `pbcopy`, `wl-copy` or `xclip` by session type and checks the display variables first, so on a headless box it fails fast rather than hanging on a clipboard nobody is listening to."
        verify: [{kind: "command", name: "cf"}]
        source: ".mi/prd/04-shell/02-aliases-utilities/prd.md"
    }

    # --------------------------------------------------------------------- git
    {
        cmd: "g"
        title: "Run git"
        use: "`g` is `git`: `g status`, `g push`, everything as usual, three characters shorter."
        topic: "git"
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["lg"]
        verify: [{kind: "alias", name: "g"}]
        source: ".mi/prd/04-shell/02-aliases-utilities/prd.md"
    }
    {
        cmd: "lg"
        title: "Open lazygit on this repository"
        use: "`lg` in any repo: staging, hunk-level commits, rebases and the log in one terminal UI. Quit with `q`."
        topic: "git"
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["g"]
        verify: [{kind: "alias", name: "lg"}]
        source: ".mi/prd/04-shell/02-aliases-utilities/prd.md"
    }

    # ---------------------------------------------------------------- terminal
    {
        cmd: "q / :q / /exit"
        title: "Leave the shell"
        use: "Any of the three exits. They exist because muscle memory arrives from vim, from a REPL, and from a chat box, and none of them should print an error."
        topic: "terminal"
        mode: "shell"
        verify: [{kind: "alias", name: "q"}, {kind: "alias", name: ":q"}, {kind: "alias", name: "/exit"}]
        source: ".mi/prd/04-shell/02-aliases-utilities/prd.md"
    }
    {
        cmd: "bb / ba"
        title: "Spawn or attach a terminal session"
        use: "`bb` spawns a session for this directory or attaches to it if one is already running; `ba` only attaches. Detached sessions survive closing the terminal."
        topic: "terminal"
        mode: "shell"
        why: "Both aliases run `brr`, not `burrito` — two different binaries exist and the shorter one is the session manager, so the name matters when you go looking for its config."
        verify: [{kind: "alias", name: "bb"}, {kind: "alias", name: "ba"}]
        source: ".mi/prd/04-shell/02-aliases-utilities/prd.md"
    }

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------ agents
    {
        cmd: "cc [...args]"
        title: "Start Claude in this directory"
        use: "`cc` launches Claude Code with permission prompts skipped, in the directory you are in. Arguments pass straight through."
        topic: "agents"
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["cr [...args]", "zc <query>"]
        why: "With a single login profile it starts immediately — no picker. The multi-login machinery only wakes up once a second profile exists, so the common case pays nothing for it."
        verify: [{kind: "command", name: "cc"}]
        source: ".mi/prd/04-shell/08-claude-launchers/prd.md"
    }
    {
        cmd: "cr [...args]"
        title: "Resume a Claude session in this directory"
        use: "`cr` is `cc --resume`: it offers the sessions from this directory and continues the one you pick."
        topic: "agents"
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["cc [...args]"]
        verify: [{kind: "command", name: "cr"}]
        source: ".mi/prd/04-shell/08-claude-launchers/prd.md"
    }
    {
        cmd: "help --json"
        title: "Read this manual as structured data"
        use: "`help --json` emits the whole manual — topics and every entry field — as one JSON document, for an agent that would rather parse than read. Plain `help` under capture is already unstyled text, so this is the optimisation, not the requirement."
        topic: "agents"
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["help", "help --md"]
        why: "The field names are an interface: renaming one is a breaking change and has to be recorded in the agent-interface PRD."
        verify: [{kind: "command", name: "help"}]
        source: ".mi/prd/06-help/05-agent-interface/prd.md"
    }
    {
        cmd: "help --md"
        title: "Write this manual out as markdown"
        use: "`help --md` prints the whole manual grouped by topic, top-to-bottom readable — for pasting into an issue or writing to a file."
        topic: "agents"
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["help --json"]
        verify: [{kind: "command", name: "help"}]
        source: ".mi/prd/06-help/05-agent-interface/prd.md"
    }
    {
        cmd: "idioms"
        title: "Use the tools this environment actually has"
        use: "Nothing to run — four rules that stop an agent guessing. Search with `rg` and find with `fd`, never `grep`/`find`. Pick with television (`tv`), not fzf. Nushell commands return structured data, so filter with `| where`, not `| grep`. And `cd` here can create the directory it is given."
        topic: "agents"
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["grep", "tv channel", "cd <path>"]
        why: "Every one of these is a failure this environment has actually seen: reaching for a tool that is not installed, or piping a table into a line-oriented filter and getting nothing."
        verify: [{kind: "prose"}]
        source: ".mi/prd/06-help/05-agent-interface/prd.md"
    }

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------ config
    {
        cmd: "help"
        title: "Read the manual for this environment"
        use: "`help` alone prints the topics and the handful of keys worth knowing first. `help <topic>` gives that topic as a nushell table, so `help find | where key =~ ctrl` composes. `help <entry>` gives one entry in full, with its why and its source PRD. Anything else is treated as a search, and `help --all` prints everything."
        topic: "config"
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["help --fuzzy", "help --check", "help --json"]
        why: "Nushell sanctions overriding `help`, which means this command also receives every `<anything> --help` in the shell — so a name it does not document is forwarded to nushell's own help untouched, and `--help` keeps working everywhere. Where a name is BOTH a nushell command and an entry here — `ls` is the live example — which one wins is not settled; the delegation PRD and this file currently disagree, and it is recorded as a correction rather than decided in the data."
        verify: [{kind: "command", name: "help"}]
        source: ".mi/prd/06-help/02-help-command/prd.md"
    }
    {
        cmd: "help --check"
        title: "Check the manual against the live configuration"
        use: "`help --check` introspects the configured shell, Neovim and WezTerm and reports three things: bindings that exist with no entry, entries whose binding is gone, and entries whose text no longer matches the live description. Non-zero exit on any of them, so it can gate a commit. It spawns editors and terminals, so it is slow on purpose and never on the path of plain `help`."
        topic: "config"
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["help"]
        why: "This is the only reason the manual can be trusted rather than hoped over. Add a binding without an entry and the check fails; a documented key that stopped existing is the dangerous case, because it sends a reader somewhere that does nothing."
        verify: [{kind: "command", name: "help"}]
        source: ".mi/prd/06-help/04-drift-check/prd.md"
    }
    {
        cmd: "rr"
        title: "Pull and apply the latest dotfiles"
        use: "`rr` is `chezmoi update --force`: fetch the dotfiles repo and apply it over `~`. Applying twice changes nothing the second time."
        topic: "config"
        mode: "shell"
        verify: [{kind: "alias", name: "rr"}]
        source: ".mi/prd/04-shell/02-aliases-utilities/prd.md"
    }
    {
        cmd: "pass <tab>"
        title: "Complete pass subcommands and entry names"
        use: "Type `pass ` and press Tab: the verbs come up, and so do the live entry names from `$PASSWORD_STORE_DIR`, without their `.gpg` suffix. Flags that are not declared still reach the real binary."
        topic: "config"
        mode: "shell"
        why: "Nushell ships no completion for pass, so this is an extern signature written by hand — which is also why an undeclared flag has to pass through rather than error."
        verify: [{kind: "command", name: "pass"}]
        source: ".mi/prd/04-shell/02-aliases-utilities/prd.md"
    }
]
